nvme: translate read/write buffers through the controller, not the block device

nvme_blk_rw() translates the I/O buffer with dev_phys_to_bus() on the block
(namespace) device.  That device has no dma-ranges, so the translation is
the identity; only the PCI controller (ns->dev->udev) carries the bridge's
inbound offset.  Admin commands (queues, PRP pool, identify) already use
the controller and work.

On a Raspberry Pi CM4 the firmware passes pcie0
  dma-ranges = <0x02000000 0x4 0x0  0x0 0x0  0x2 0x0>
(PCIe bus 0x4_0000_0000 = CPU 0), so every read DMA'd to an untranslated
address: "nvme read" reported OK but a buffer pre-filled with 0xa5 was
unchanged and "part list" found no partition table (E9b-3, 2026-10-20,
trbarbour/NixOS-boot-image#316).  Not fixed upstream as of main e36b675ebc
(2026-10-19).

--- a/drivers/nvme/nvme.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/nvme.c
@@ -750,7 +750,13 @@
 	u64 prp2;
 	u64 total_len = blkcnt << desc->log2blksz;
 	u64 temp_len = total_len;
-	uintptr_t dma_addr = dev_phys_to_bus(udev, (uintptr_t)buffer);
+	/*
+	 * Translate through the controller: the block device has no
+	 * dma-ranges, so its translation is the identity, which is wrong
+	 * behind a bridge with an inbound offset (BCM2711: PCIe bus address
+	 * 0x4_0000_0000 is CPU address 0).
+	 */
+	uintptr_t dma_addr = dev_phys_to_bus(dev->udev, (uintptr_t)buffer);
 
 	u64 slba = blknr;
 	u16 lbas = 1 << (dev->max_transfer_shift - ns->lba_shift);
